## Workspace Setup Protocol (MANDATORY before spawning any sub-agent)

Chief must be able to watch every sub-agent in real time. This requires sub-agents to be launched **inside tmux panes via `tmux send-keys`**, not via the Agent tool. The Agent tool runs agents as background tasks with no tmux presence — Chief cannot observe them.

### §0. Pre-Flight Checklist

Before spawning any agents, lead-dev MUST:

1. Confirm the active tmux session name (e.g. `[PROJECT_SLUG]`).
2. Create the required panes.
3. Set up git worktrees for each sub-agent.
4. Launch each sub-agent into its pane via `tmux send-keys`.
5. Brief each sub-agent via `tmux send-keys` after it starts.

### §0.1 Pane Layout (2 sub-agents)

```bash
# Step 1 — create panes (run from lead-dev's pane, session name e.g. "[PROJECT_SLUG]")
tmux split-window -h -t [SESSION]           # right pane: sub-agent-1
tmux split-window -v -t [SESSION]:0.1       # splits right into top/bottom: sub-agent-2

# Step 2 — create isolated git worktrees
git -C [PROJECT_ROOT] worktree add .worktrees/[agent-1-name] -b [branch-1]
git -C [PROJECT_ROOT] worktree add .worktrees/[agent-2-name] -b [branch-2]

# Step 3 — launch agents into their panes (THIS is what makes activity visible to Chief)
tmux send-keys -t [SESSION]:0.1 "cd [PROJECT_ROOT]/.worktrees/[agent-1-name] && claude --permission-mode auto" Enter
tmux send-keys -t [SESSION]:0.2 "cd [PROJECT_ROOT]/.worktrees/[agent-2-name] && claude --permission-mode auto" Enter

# Step 4 — wait ~5s for agents to start, then send briefs
sleep 5
tmux send-keys -t [SESSION]:0.1 "[agent-1 brief]" Enter
tmux send-keys -t [SESSION]:0.2 "[agent-2 brief]" Enter
```

For 3 sub-agents, add a third pane:
```bash
tmux split-window -v -t [SESSION]:0.2       # splits bottom-right again
git -C [PROJECT_ROOT] worktree add .worktrees/[agent-3-name] -b [branch-3]
tmux send-keys -t [SESSION]:0.3 "cd [PROJECT_ROOT]/.worktrees/[agent-3-name] && claude --permission-mode auto" Enter
```

### §0.2 Agent Brief Format (sent via tmux send-keys)

Each brief sent to a sub-agent pane must include:

```
You are [agent-role] on the [PROJECT_NAME] agentic team.
Lead-dev is your only contact — do NOT message Chief directly.
Your worktree: [absolute path to worktree]
Your branch: [branch-name]

Constitution: read CLAUDE.md in your worktree before starting.
RED ZONE files (never touch): [LIST_RED_ZONE_FILES_AND_DIRS]

Your tasks:
- [TASK-ID]: [description] [ZONE]

Hard rules:
- NEVER push to main
- ALWAYS follow SACRED Workflow for every commit
- [ADD_PROJECT_SPECIFIC_RULES]

When done, output your report in this pane. Lead-dev will collect it.
```

### §0.3 Sub-Agent Permission Mode

Sub-agents are launched with `--permission-mode auto`:

```bash
tmux send-keys -t [SESSION]:0.1 "cd [PROJECT_ROOT]/.worktrees/[agent-name] && claude --permission-mode auto" Enter
```

**Why `--permission-mode auto`:**
- Auto mode approves operations that match the project's settings allowlist (`.claude/settings.local.json`) without prompting. This covers all routine GREEN ZONE operations: file reads, bash commands, git operations, tests, linting, formatting.
- Operations NOT on the allowlist still surface for approval. Anything novel or potentially destructive gets a prompt — the Lead reviews it, not Chief.
- The Lead acts as the approval layer for sub-agent work. Chief's time is not spent approving every `Read` call.

**Why NOT `--dangerously-skip-permissions`:**
- Bypasses ALL permission checks with no exceptions — including RED ZONE files. Removes the harness as any kind of safety net.
- FORBIDDEN for sub-agents. May only be used by Chief, and only when Chief has independently verified the full scope of actions the session will take.

**Why NOT plain `claude` (no flags):**
- Standard mode prompts on every unlisted tool call. Sub-agents stall constantly on routine operations, requiring Chief to approve things the Lead is already authorised to approve. The agentic model breaks.

**The allowlist is Chief's standing pre-approval.** The `.claude/settings.local.json` allowlist covers the routine operation set for this project. Adding an operation to the allowlist is the correct way to extend sub-agent authority — not escalating to `--dangerously-skip-permissions`.

**RED ZONE defence:** RED ZONE file paths are not on the allowlist. Any sub-agent attempt to touch them will surface as an unapproved operation in auto mode — the Lead catches it before it reaches Chief.

### §0.4 Why NOT the Agent Tool for Observable Work

The Agent tool (`Agent(...)`) runs agents as background tasks. Their output goes to a log — Chief cannot see it in real time. Use the Agent tool only when:
- The task is a one-shot research query where live observation is not needed, OR
- Chief has explicitly confirmed that background-only operation is acceptable.

For any session where Chief wants to observe sub-agent work in real time, always use the `tmux send-keys ... claude --permission-mode auto` pattern above.

